-- title:
--   Artin induction in characteristic p for additive invariants
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, $G$ a finite group, and $\psi$ an arbitrary integer-valued function on the objects of $\mathrm{Rep}_{\mathbb{Z}/p}(G)$, the category of representations of $G$ over $\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$. Two hypotheses are imposed on $\psi$. First, additivity: for every short complex $X$ of such representations that is short exact, with middle term $X.X_2$ finite-dimensional over $\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$, one has $\psi(X.X_2) = \psi(X.X_1) + \psi(X.X_3)$. Second, vanishing on inductions from cyclic $p'$-subgroups: for every subgroup $H \le G$ which is cyclic and whose order $\#H$ is coprime to $p$, and every finite-dimensional representation $N$ of $H$ over $\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$, the induced representation along the inclusion $H \hookrightarrow G$ satisfies $\psi(\mathrm{Ind}_H^G N) = 0$. The conclusion is that $\psi(M) = 0$ for every representation $M$ of $G$ over $\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$ that is finite-dimensional over $\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$. Note that additivity is required only for short exact sequences whose middle term is finite-dimensional, and that no hypothesis is placed on $\psi$ at infinite-dimensional representations.
--
--   This is Artin's induction theorem in the form used to reduce computations of Euler characteristics of $\mathbb{F}_p[G]$-modules to induced modules from cyclic subgroups of order prime to $p$: rationally, the representation ring is generated by such inductions, and $\mathbb{Z}$ is torsion-free. It is used in the proof of the local Euler-characteristic formula underlying the count of invariants and of a second continuous cohomology group, [`groupCohomology.finrank_invariants_add_finrank_continuousH2S_add_finrank_eq_of_ne_two`](thm.html#groupCohomology.finrank_invariants_add_finrank_continuousH2S_add_finrank_eq_of_ne_two).
